=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
After exhaustive examination of all five FRR devices and both hosts, the network is currently operating correctly. End-to-end ICMP reachability from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is fully functional in both directions, confirmed by 4/4 ping success and clean traceroutes in both directions (forward path: leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_0 → leaf_router_0_1; reverse: the same path inverted). All eight BGP sessions are in Established state with zero drops since lab startup, all routing tables on every device correctly contain both the 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 prefixes, the kernel FIB on leaf_router_0_1 matches the FRR RIB, and interface counters show zero errors or drops on all links including leaf_router_0_1's eth0, eth1, and eth2.

One structural BGP observation is worth noting: spine_router_0_0 (AS 65100) consistently shows PfxRcd=0 from super_spine_router_0. This occurs because the super_spine always selects spine_router_0_0's paths as best (lower router-ID 172.16.0.1 vs 172.16.0.3) and re-advertises them back to spine_router_0_0 with AS 65100 in the AS-path, which spine_router_0_0 drops per BGP loop-prevention rules. This is a standard BGP behavior in a single-pod CLOS topology — it does not blackhole any traffic because both spines learn all leaf prefixes directly from the leaves, and backup paths remain available via the alternate spine whenever the primary path fails. The user's reported "cut off" appears to have been transient — most likely a sub-second window during initial BGP convergence at lab startup (the coalesce timer fires at ~1.1 s) — and has fully self-resolved. No configuration change is required.
=== END ===